what do I look for in a role I think I  look for things that interest me I think  I look for things that I don't quite  understand and things that I want to  explore I look for things to be  difficult I don't want it to be easy and  if it feels like I know exactly how to  do it as soon as I read it I'm not  really that interested it's always got  to kind of be a challenge I'm looking  for something a bit different from what  I have been doing usually in a role and  I'm looking but I'm mainly looking for  truth and integrity even um no matter  what it is even if it's a comic cameo  there's still got to be a truth behind  it and I like the whole it not just I'm  not just looking at my character obvious  I'm looking at the whole thing I like I  like the thought that it works for me  it's it's really exciting if you you  know you have a character that has an  arc that you know learn something you  know I like roles where you get a chance  to transform you know not just  physically but you know emotionally and  to be able to play a strong female  character I'm pretty bad at reading  scripts although I do a lot of it let's  say you think that I would have got  better but I'm not brilliant at it so I  take a lot of advice I'll get other  people to and to read stuff as well you  know if they have the time and the  inclination also a pretty good judge is  if I'm reading the script and I don't  want to either throw it at the wall or  flush it down the toilet or just put it  down if I keep reading it then that's a  pretty good indicator and then after  that it's who's going to direct it who's  going to be in it and that sort of thing  and where is it going to be shot in  terms of preparing it really depends on  the role and I think the director and  the type of material if if it's a period  piece for example I try and do research  about the period you know look at  historical material but I got into a lot  of research with a foe I did called  Dangerous Method and then I didn't  actually all around that period it was  very very research heavy so it would be  whatever the thing was bay  Starlin then I'd go off on a tangent and  read just absolutely everything from  technically I just go over and over and  over and over and over the script as  many times as possible to try make the  dialogue sort of muscle memory the older  I get the more prepared I feel I need to  be on every level I never used to learn  lines until the night before but now I  don't do that anymore they have to be  committed to long-term memory as opposed  to short-term memory generally speaking  that you can't always do that but that's  right I try to do so I usually have a  good grasp of the script and I read the  script a number of times and I try and  break the script down into a timeline to  see if I understand the beginning in the  middle and the end and also if there are  flashbacks is where I get quite confused  so I'll you know I'll do a couple of  timelines to see if I if it all makes  sense to me so that I can track there  the journey you want to know who the  person is even though you might only be  in five minutes of the script that day  so I will do my own rehearsals as if I  was rehearsing with the director really  on my own I did a movie last year called  beyond the lights where I had a lot of  singing and dancing and so that became  part of the preparation you know  watching music videos having singing  lessons dance training and obviously  that's not required for every role so if  I can I'll go and meet people who do the  particular job of the character that I'm  being asked to play I mean if it's a if  it's a period piece that's slightly more  difficult I'll generally read around the  topic if it's a writer who's read out  their written other stuff then I would  you know go and look at their other  material in terms of a successful  collaboration between actor and director  I think it's really about understanding  the vision of the director understanding  the point of view also listening it's  sort of so simple but it's such a key  thing in any sort of artistic creative  collaboration the directors I've loved  most have been the ones who are very  free who are open to people's  suggestions no matter who it is people  like Steven Daldry he'd suddenly say  let's just shoot another scene here now  let's why don't we you know let's make  let's say they're in this room and this  happens  we'll just choose another scene that we  would talk about in the cabin and then  just do it yeah I just loved that I  don't think that I could talk about  directors and not talk about Joe right  because we've done three films together  you know and I think that collaboration  has to be the most important of my  career we have a working relationship  that isn't necessarily comfortable but  it is quite challenging is always  supportive but it's not like but we've  got this weird thing what we sort of  don't need to speak to each other so  even when we're disagreeing it's not  like we're really doing it verbally  we're sort of sort of doing it with mime  and looking at something you know and I  think particularly when I first sort of  started working at with him it was a  time when I was getting a lot of  criticism from everywhere and he was  sort of the first person that said to me  you can really do this and you need to  work hard and put your head down and so  obviously you know I've always hugely  appreciated him for that I don't like  being over directed some people feel  that they have to be seen to be  directing you know and they come in and  give you a note after every thing and  because you're the only thing you've got  your instincts so if there and they can  be interfered with sometimes rather than  nurtured and I find that to be then you  start to doubt them when I work with  Stephen Frears on tomorrow drew I  suddenly realized when I saw how he  works with all his other crew that he  just brings people in who he trusts he  entrusts you to do your job he doesn't  you know ask you did your job if he  doesn't think you can't do it he thinks  you can't do it and so that was really  wonderful to go oh I see you're just  you're just letting me do what I can do  and it's it that's really exciting to  know that someone goes yeah you know I  trust you pearls of wisdom I don't know  if I feel experienced enough to have  great wisdom I mean for me it's really  been a case of working hard and being  determined  I tell make sure you really want it  because it's a hard road if you don't  there's a lot of rejection you've got to  really believe in yourself we've got a  poster on our on our kitchen wall which  is a painting in the National Portrait  Gallery of the back of Samuel Beckett's  head  watching a production of Waiting for  Godot and on the bottom it's a thing  that he used to say which was no matter  try again fail again fail better we must  be more brave about failing and of  dealing with rejection the rejection is  not personal it's not personal it's  necessary yeah it's a really hard  profession you know I mean you've got to  love it  you've got an in it is a vocation  it doesn't like of it's not a vocation  because you are going to get trampled  down you are going to get people saying  no and and you have to have that thing  where you can't do anything else because  then you know then you are an actor so I  think it's you know keep on keep on keep  on keep on and also get a job I would  say that to two young actors starting  out is get a normal job a real job that  you can work around auditions and  whatever so that you're hanging out with  real people because that's those are the  people you were hopefully going to  people training unless you're you know  the sort of person that's going to  portray a lot of serial killers if you  become famous don't believe any of the  hype because that's all it is really  it's all a mirage you know just believe  in yourself
 

